# Draco — Universal GPU + CPU LLM Runtime

**Version 0.8.0** | 821 GPU-path tests passing | 69 native-CPU-runtime + GPU-memory-utility tests passing | dual-backend `LLM(backend=...)` — `transformers` (default) or `draco` (native) | 13 model adapters | 3 CPU-executor architecture families (RoPE/RMSNorm/gated, learned-pos/LayerNorm/fused-QKV, ALiBi) | 5 CPU quantization/storage formats | 5 benchmark modes | OpenAI server | PagedAttention | Pipeline Parallelism | Health Checks | Production Benchmarks | Native CPU Runtime | `.draco` format | GPU OOM-avoidance

A plugin/registry-based inference runtime for HuggingFace causal language models, with two execution backends sharing one model format and one scheduler:

- **GPU backend** — the original vLLM-compatible engine: PagedAttention, tensor/pipeline parallelism, continuous batching, speculative decoding, an OpenAI-compatible server.
- **Native CPU backend** — no PyTorch in the hot path. A self-contained, memory-mapped `.draco` model format (conceptually: llama.cpp + GGUF, built natively inside Draco) with CPU-native INT8/INT4 weight quantization, a pure-numpy execution graph, its own KV cache, continuous batching (reusing the same scheduler as the GPU path), and adaptive draft-model-free speculative decoding.

`device="auto"` picks between them based on detected hardware and the model's own metadata — see [Native CPU Runtime](#native-cpu-runtime).

### Which model-loading backend: `transformers` vs `draco`

`LLM(...)` also has a separate, higher-level `backend` switch — independent from the CPU/GPU `device` choice above — that decides how the model is loaded and run:

```python
from draco import LLM

# Default: backend="transformers" — delegates model loading and the whole
# generate() loop to the installed transformers library. Supports whatever
# architectures your transformers version supports (text-only or
# text+vision "ForConditionalGeneration" models), with transformers' own
# correct, KV-cached generation. Use this unless you have a specific
# reason not to.
llm = LLM(model="Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct")

# Explicit opt-in: Draco's own native CPU/GPU engine and model adapters
# (MODEL_REGISTRY). Only architectures registered there are supported —
# unsupported ones raise ModelNotFoundError with a suggestion to drop
# back to backend="transformers".
llm = LLM(model="Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct", backend="draco")
```

`backend="transformers"` is the default specifically because it is the reliable path: it supports any architecture your installed transformers version does (via an `AutoModelForCausalLM` → `AutoModelForImageTextToText` → `AutoModelForVision2Seq` → `AutoModel` fallback chain, so text+vision models load too, not just text-only ones), and it reuses transformers' own `.generate()` — which handles KV caching and position ids correctly out of the box. `backend="draco"` gives you Draco's own adapters, which is where architecture support and the CPU/GPU device choice above apply; it is more limited (25 registered architectures) and, prior to v0.8.0, had a real correctness bug in its no-KV-cache generation loop (fixed — see [Known Limitations](#known-limitations)).

```
python demo.py            # Run all 38 demo sections
python demo.py 1 3 5      # Run specific sections
python -m pytest tests/ -v  # Run the GPU-path test suite (needs torch)
python -m pytest tests/format tests/runtime_cpu -v  # Native CPU runtime (numpy only, no torch needed)
```

