## Native CPU Runtime

A second execution backend that runs entirely without PyTorch, built around one new artifact: the `.draco` file.

### The `.draco` format

A versioned, self-contained, memory-mapped model container — not a JSON pointer to safetensors, not a zip of the original HF files. `LLM(model="model.draco")` needs nothing else at inference time: architecture metadata, tokenizer (optional), tensor index, and tensor data all live in one file, opened via `mmap` so the OS page cache — not Python — controls what's actually resident in RAM.

```python
from draco.format import DracoWriter, DracoReader

writer = DracoWriter("model.draco")
writer.add_metadata({"architecture": "LlamaForCausalLM", "model_type": "llama", ...})
writer.add_tensor("layers.0.self_attn.q_proj.weight", weight, quantization="int8_sym")
writer.finalize()  # atomic: tmp file -> self-validate -> rename; a failed write never
                   # leaves a corrupt file at the target path

reader = DracoReader("model.draco")
print(reader.architecture, reader.tensor_names())
```

```bash
draco convert --model meta-llama/Llama-3-8B --output Llama-3-8B.draco --quantization int8
draco inspect Llama-3-8B.draco     # architecture, quantization, tensor count, CPU kernel info
draco validate Llama-3-8B.draco    # header/offset/bounds/checksum validation, never segfaults on corrupt input
draco system-info                  # detected ISA (AVX2/AVX512/VNNI/AMX/NEON), thread plan, device="auto" resolution
```

### CPU-native quantization and storage formats

Five formats, each with a real numpy codec and round-trip/matmul correctness tests — no format is ever exposed without a working implementation:

| Format | Kind | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `int8_sym` | weight-only quant | per-row symmetric, 1 byte/weight |
| `int8_asym` | weight-only quant | per-row asymmetric (zero-point) — lower error than symmetric on off-center weight distributions |
| `int4_groupwise` | weight-only quant | groupwise asymmetric, 2 values packed per byte |
| `f16` | storage dtype | native numpy half precision |
| `bf16` | storage dtype | truncated-mantissa float32 (round-to-nearest-even), no native numpy dtype so stored as raw bit patterns — half the size of f32 with f32's full exponent range (unlike f16, which can overflow on values outside its narrower range) |

### Full execution graph, generalized across architecture families

`draco.runtime.cpu.executor.CPUExecutionBackend` reads architecture-shape metadata from the `.draco` file (norm type, position-encoding type, MLP type, fused-vs-separate QKV, bias placement) rather than hardcoding one model family. Three families are implemented and each verified against an **independently-written plain-numpy reference forward pass** (not just "didn't crash"):

| Family | Norm | Position encoding | MLP | QKV | Example models |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Llama-style | RMSNorm | RoPE | Gated (SwiGLU) | separate q/k/v | Llama, Qwen2, Mistral |
| GPT-2-style | LayerNorm (+bias) | Learned embedding | Standard (GELU) | fused `c_attn` | GPT-2 |
| BLOOM-style | LayerNorm (+bias) | ALiBi | Standard (GELU) | separate q/k/v | BLOOM |

This is not a claim that all 13 GPU-side model adapters run correctly on the CPU path today — attention variants like Falcon's parallel attention+MLP block or true multi-query (single shared KV head) aren't wired in — but it's real, tested breadth across the three largest architectural axes (normalization, position encoding, MLP gating), not just the one family from the previous version.

```python
from draco.runtime.cpu.llm import CPUNativeLLM

llm = CPUNativeLLM("model.draco", memory_utilization=0.85)
tokens = llm.generate([1, 2, 3], max_new_tokens=64)
print(llm.runtime_info())
```

### Continuous batching (shared scheduler, not forked)

The CPU path reuses `draco.scheduler.scheduler.ContinuousBatchScheduler` — the same request-admission bookkeeping the GPU engine uses — rather than a CPU-specific copy. `CPUBatchEngine` turns scheduled batches into prefill calls for newly-admitted sequences and a single vectorized decode call (`forward_batch_decode`) across all running sequences per step, admitting/evicting sequences from the KV cache as they start and finish.

```python
llm = CPUNativeLLM("model.draco")
results = llm.generate_batch([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5], [6, 7, 8, 9]], max_new_tokens=64)
```

### Adaptive speculative decoding (draft-model-free)

The GPU engine's speculative decoder (`draco.engine.speculative`) uses a separate draft model. The CPU path can't assume one is available, so `draco.runtime.cpu.speculative.AdaptiveSpeculativeDecoder` uses **prompt-lookup decoding**: it drafts candidate continuations by matching the last few generated tokens against earlier context (n-gram lookup, no second model needed), verifies the whole draft in one forward pass, and rolls back the KV cache for any rejected suffix. This is exact for greedy decoding — the output is token-for-token identical to plain autoregressive generation, just potentially faster on repetitive continuations (code, quoted text, structured output). Draft length adapts via an EMA of the acceptance rate: it grows after a run of good guesses and shrinks after a run of bad ones.

```python
tokens = llm.generate([1, 2, 3], max_new_tokens=64, speculative=True)
```

### Automatic memory management and device selection

`draco.runtime.cpu.mem_util` detects total/available host RAM (`/proc/meminfo`) and derives a usable budget as `available × cpu_memory_utilization` (default 0.85) — the same idea as `gpu_memory_utilization` on the GPU path, but for system RAM. `draco.convert.planner.CPUQuantizationPlanner` walks quantization options from most-accurate-that-fits down given that budget, model size, and context/concurrency targets, and raises `InsufficientMemoryError` (rather than silently OOMing) when nothing fits. `draco.runtime.device_manager.resolve_device("auto", ...)` picks CPU vs. CUDA based on detected hardware and any CPU-oriented metadata embedded in the `.draco` file.

### GPU-side memory planning and OOM avoidance

Two additive utilities that sit alongside the existing GPU engine (`draco.engine.llm.LLM`) without modifying it — this environment has no GPU to validate engine changes against, so the engine itself is untouched, but these are independently testable:

- `draco.runtime.gpu.memory.GPUMemoryPlanner` — the GPU-side counterpart to `CPUMemoryPlanner`. `auto_memory_budget()` mirrors `gpu_memory_utilization` (detects free CUDA memory via `torch.cuda.mem_get_info`, budgets a fraction of it). `max_num_seqs_that_fit()` is the actual **avoidance** mechanism: solve directly for how many concurrent sequences' worth of KV cache fit in the remaining budget after weights are loaded, so a scheduler can refuse admission past that number instead of admitting-and-hoping.
- `draco.utils.oom_guard.run_with_oom_backoff()` — the **recovery** mechanism for when an OOM happens anyway (misestimated size, fragmentation, a concurrent process). Catches OOM-like errors (by exception type name/message, so it doesn't need a specific torch version's exception class), clears the CUDA allocator cache, backs off a memory-fraction keyword argument geometrically, and retries up to a bounded number of attempts — tested with mocked flaky/always-failing callables since there's no real GPU here to trigger a genuine OOM against.

```python
from draco.utils.oom_guard import run_with_oom_backoff, OOMRetryConfig

result = run_with_oom_backoff(
    lambda gpu_memory_utilization: my_llm.generate(prompt, gpu_memory_utilization=gpu_memory_utilization),
    initial_value=0.9,
    config=OOMRetryConfig(max_retries=3, backoff_factor=0.7, min_value=0.3),
)
```

### What's real vs. reference-tier, honestly

Everything above is implemented and tested end-to-end (`tests/format/`, `tests/runtime_cpu/`, `tests/runtime_gpu/`, `tests/utils/` — 69 tests total, including three independent-reference-implementation cross-checks across the three architecture families, a test that continuous-batched generation exactly matches single-sequence generation, a test that ALiBi position bias stays correct through the batched decode path specifically, and a test that speculative decoding is token-for-token lossless vs. plain greedy decoding). What's **not** built: ISA-specific CPU kernels (AVX2/AVX512/VNNI/AMX/NEON intrinsics) — the kernel dispatcher detects real CPU capabilities but only ever selects the portable numpy "generic" tier, and says so explicitly (`kernel_downgraded` in `runtime_info()`) rather than claiming a fast path that isn't there. Real ISA kernels need a compiled extension validated on real hardware, which this environment can't provide — same reason no GPU engine code was modified this round: there's no CUDA device here to test against, so the GPU-side additions are memory-planning/OOM-recovery utilities that don't require touching the (already-passing) existing engine.

