## Known Limitations

- `LLM(...)` defaults to `backend="transformers"` (v0.8.0+) specifically because Draco's own native engine (`backend="draco"`) has real, known gaps documented below — use `backend="draco"` deliberately, not as a default
- Draco's native engine (`backend="draco"`) has no per-adapter KV cache: `_generate_single` and `StreamingGenerator.stream` re-feed the full sequence every decode step (fixed a real bug where feeding only the latest token — combined with adapters recomputing `position_ids` from zero every call — produced degenerate/repeating output after the first step or two). Both paths are now validated against a real model (Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct on CPU): responses stop naturally and are coherent
- `backend="draco"` generation is O(n²) in sequence length (no KV cache reuse) until the existing paged-KV-cache machinery (`draco/kv_cache/block.py`, `draco/scheduler/scheduler.py`) is actually wired into `ModelExecutor.forward()` — that wiring is a separate, larger undertaking, not done in this pass
- For instruction-tuned models (Qwen2.5, Llama-3, ...) the native `generate()` path now applies the tokenizer's chat template automatically (single user turn, `add_generation_prompt=True`) so generation starts from the "assistant" turn and stops on the model's chat end token (e.g. `<|im_end|>`), and stops on every EOS id declared in `generation_config.json` — raw-text completion on an instruct model requires passing an already-formatted prompt (any prompt containing a special-token string is left untouched)
- `backend="draco"` only supports the 25 architectures registered in `MODEL_REGISTRY`; newer or multimodal ("ForConditionalGeneration") architectures raise `ModelNotFoundError` there — use the default `backend="transformers"` for those
- No GPU required for tests/demo, but real inference requires CUDA 11.8+ and compute capability >= 7.0
- No pipeline parallelism or NCCL multi-GPU communication (tensor parallelism done)
- Quantization backends require external libraries (auto-gptq, autoawq, bitsandbytes)
- GPT-2, Falcon, and BLOOM adapters use non-RoPE position encodings (learned/ALiBi) — no long-context extension
- OPT, Baichuan, Yi, and other architectures not yet supported
- Weight loading is CPU-first — no direct GPU memory-mapped loading
- Benchmark modes are CPU/simulated — real GPU benchmarks require model weights
- CUDA kernels are reference PyTorch implementations — custom GPU kernels not yet built
- Native CPU runtime (`.draco`) only has the portable numpy kernel tier — no AVX2/AVX512/AMX-optimized GEMM yet, so it is not expected to be faster than llama.cpp/GGUF; the format, quantization, execution graph, batching, and speculative decoding are real and tested, the raw kernel speed is not yet optimized
- Native CPU runtime supports 3 architecture families (Llama/Qwen2/Mistral-style RoPE+RMSNorm+gated-MLP, GPT-2-style learned-position+LayerNorm+fused-QKV, BLOOM-style ALiBi+LayerNorm) — not all 13 GPU-side adapters' exact attention/MLP variants (e.g. Falcon's parallel attention+MLP, true multi-query attention) are wired into the CPU executor yet
- Native CPU speculative decoding is draft-model-free (prompt-lookup only) — it helps on repetitive continuations and does nothing extra on novel text (falls back to plain greedy for that step, still correct)
- GPU memory planning (`GPUMemoryPlanner`) and OOM-recovery (`run_with_oom_backoff`) are implemented and unit-tested with mocked failures, but not validated against a real GPU/real OOM condition — no CUDA device is available in this development environment

